Subject: Scopes measure what they show, and say what it is

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-98). The colour scopes always read the SDR
preview canvas and labelled it "sRGB / Rec.709", whatever the project.

- An HDR project (FL-97: it holds a PQ or HLG source) is measured, while
  paused, from its float composite converted explicitly to BT.2100 PQ in
  BT.2020, with BT.2020 coefficients, a cd/m² graticule (along the axis
  for the histogram) and BT.2111 PQ bar targets. The preview registers
  its colour management, resolved from the renderer's own inputs, and a
  capture (`captureFrameSignal`) on the live scope renderer, which draws
  titles the preview overlays in the DOM and never touches the preview's
  canvas, frame cache or render lock. A paused sample is reused until
  its frame, graph or colour inputs change. While playing, or when the
  float composite cannot be read, the scopes read the display and say
  so (tone-mapped or clipped by the project's SDR monitoring).
- The render engine's signal capture no longer answers from the frame
  cache (it failed on every cached paused frame), no longer draws or
  caches the display canvas (it stored a background-only frame), and
  reuses its conversion target and readback buffer.
- The header names the colour space measured and the paused frame
  evaluated; a sample captured before playback jumps (a seek or loop
  while playing) is dropped.
- The graticule overlay, the CPU fallback and the GPU vectorscope share
  one geometry: 100% ring at 0.46 of the size, bar targets computed from
  the matrix and bar level in use (they were drawn up to 11 units off),
  skin line at 123°. The GPU waveform's IRE grid is left to the overlay
  on PQ.
- The GPU waveform filled only every n-th column for a source narrower
  than its 1024 columns (preview samples, float frames), so the render
  could miss the trace; each source pixel now fills exactly its columns.
- The CPU scope math moves to `preview/utils/scope-math.ts`, where
  reference ramps and bars test it exactly.

